votes.reasoning:
The refactor introduces a clear defect in `refine(gsMatrix<T> const & boxes)`. The original code converted both corners of the box:

```cpp
k1[j] = m_uIndices[fLevel][j][k1[j]];
k2[j] = m_uIndices[fLevel][j][k2[j]];
```

The replacement converts `k2` twice and never converts `k1`:

```cpp
_knotIndexToDiadicIndex(fLevel,k2);
_knotIndexToDiadicIndex(fLevel,k2);
```

This causes two problems when `m_manualLevels` is true:

1. **`k1` is never converted.** It stays a knot index while `k2` becomes a dyadic index. `m_tree.sinkBox(k1, k2, fLevel)` then gets a box whose corners are in different index spaces, so the wrong region is refined.
2. **`k2` is mapped twice.** The second pass treats a dyadic index as a knot index and looks it up again in `m_uIndices[fLevel][r]`. Dyadic indices are generally larger than knot indices, so this produces wrong values. It can also read past the end of the vector, which is undefined behavior. In release builds there is no assert to catch this, because the single-index helper only checks `m_manualLevels`.

The intended code is clearly:

```cpp
_knotIndexToDiadicIndex(fLevel,k1);
_knotIndexToDiadicIndex(fLevel,k2);
```

The new helper functions (`_knotIndexToDiadicIndex` and `_diadicIndexToKnotIndex`, scalar and vector versions) look reasonable in themselves. The bug is only at the call site in `refine`, and it changes behavior for every manual-level hierarchical basis.

